IV. HYPOTHESIS

A proposition becomes a hypothesis when it is explicitly presented as something to be tested.

PROPOSITION
     ↓
HYPOTHESIS

For example:

> H₁: The ambiguity is caused by policy condition X.



But immediately:

> H₀: The ambiguity is not caused by X; another factor explains the observations.



The system therefore refuses a one-hypothesis universe.


---

V. THE COUNTERHYPOTHESIS

Every material hypothesis SHALL have credible alternatives.

H₁
│
├── H₀
├── H₂
├── H₃
└── UNKNOWN

For example:

H₁ → policy ambiguity
H₂ → implementation defect
H₃ → temporal/version drift
H₄ → environmental change
H₅ → measurement artifact
H₀ → apparent pattern is non-causal

This is crucial.

THE ENGINE DOES NOT ASK ONLY WHETHER H₁ CAN BE TRUE.

IT ASKS WHETHER H₁ EXPLAINS THE EVIDENCE BETTER THAN ITS ALTERNATIVES.


---

VI. THE UNKNOWN HYPOTHESIS

There must always be room for:

UNKNOWN

Why?

Because forcing the system to choose among known explanations can itself manufacture false certainty.

assert!(unknown_is_a_valid_hypothesis_state());
assert!(unknown_is_not_silently_converted_to_failure());

Thus:

H₁
H₂
H₃
UNKNOWN

can legitimately coexist during investigation.


